import{_ as d,C as n,o as c,c as m,j as t,a,E as o,a2 as i}from"./chunks/framework.CdvW4CUT.js";const b=JSON.parse('{"title":"Sync the memory","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"Sync the memory"},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/agent/synchroniser.md","filePath":"en/agent/synchroniser.md"}'),l={name:"en/agent/synchroniser.md"};function g(u,e,p,y,f,w){const s=n("AskExample"),r=n("Ask"),h=n("Capture");return c(),m("div",null,[e[0]||(e[0]=t("h1",{id:"sync-the-memory",tabindex:"-1"},[a("Sync the memory "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#sync-the-memory","aria-label":'Permalink to "Sync the memory"'},"​")],-1)),e[1]||(e[1]=t("p",null,"This journey keeps the memory up to date across your machines, with git: see where the repository stands, sync, commit your changes, resolve a conflict. The application does it in one click; the agent does it with four tools, with safeguards.",-1)),e[2]||(e[2]=t("h2",{id:"what-you-ask-the-agent",tabindex:"-1"},[a("What you ask the agent "),t("a",{class:"header-anchor",href:"#what-you-ask-the-agent","aria-label":'Permalink to "What you ask the agent"'},"​")],-1)),o(s,{tool:"mem_git_status"}),o(s,{tool:"mem_git_sync"}),o(s,{tool:"mem_git_commit"}),o(r,{say:"There is a conflict with the remote: show me the three versions of each file.",verb:"mem_git_resolve",family:"git"}),e[3]||(e[3]=i('<p>Syncing is not automatic: the agent calls <code>mem_git_sync</code> when you ask (&quot;sync the memory&quot;, &quot;where is git at?&quot;) or when it judges it useful. The delivered procedures (see <a href="/en/agent/connecter.html">Connect the agent</a>) invite it to do so at the start of a session, with the resume, and at the end of a session, with the archive; an agent that does not follow them will not do it on its own.</p><h2 id="what-the-agent-does" tabindex="-1">What the agent does <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-agent-does" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the agent does&quot;">​</a></h2><ol><li><p><a href="/en/reference/mcp/git.html#mem-git-status"><code>mem_git_status</code></a> returns the repository state: branch, remote (without credentials), ahead and behind, uncommitted changes, operation in progress, files carrying conflict markers, state of the Obsidian Git plugin. It writes nothing and uses <strong>no network</strong> by default; <code>fetch: true</code> queries the remote first. If git is not found, the state says so with the installation command, and the agent relays it to you without running it.</p></li><li><p><a href="/en/reference/mcp/git.html#mem-git-sync"><code>mem_git_sync</code></a> (no parameters) fetches, rebases your local commits if the remote moved, then pushes. <strong>Never forced, never an automatic stash.</strong> A real conflict <strong>aborts</strong> the rebase: the repository goes back to its previous state, no marker is left, and the files involved are returned. It is refused if uncommitted changes prevent the rebase.</p></li><li><p><a href="/en/reference/mcp/git.html#mem-git-commit"><code>mem_git_commit</code></a> commits the uncommitted changes (<code>paths</code> for only some, otherwise all) with <code>message</code> (non-empty, 1,000 characters at most), then pushes (<code>push: false</code> to skip). <strong>It is to be called only with the user&#39;s explicit agreement</strong>: those changes are yours. It refuses a file that carries conflict markers, unless <code>allow_markers: true</code> for a file that merely quotes them. The <strong>AI agent</strong> origin&#39;s template can only surround the message, which is always kept: see <a href="#the-commit-message-template">The commit message template</a>.</p></li><li><p><a href="/en/reference/mcp/git.html#mem-git-resolve"><code>mem_git_resolve</code></a> resolves a <strong>real</strong> conflict, in two steps:</p><ul><li><strong>without <code>resolutions</code></strong>, it inspects: for each file in conflict it returns its three versions (<code>base</code>, <code>local</code>, <code>distant</code>) and an <code>empreinte</code>, then aborts: the repository does not change;</li><li><strong>with <code>resolutions</code></strong> (the resolved text of each file, or <code>keep: &quot;local&quot;</code> or <code>&quot;distant&quot;</code>) and the <code>empreinte</code> returned (handed back in <code>fingerprint</code>), it redoes the merge, writes the texts, commits and pushes. If the repository or the remote moved in between, nothing is done; no marker is ever accepted or left.</li></ul><p>The catalog asks for it to be used <strong>only with the user&#39;s agreement on the merged text</strong>.</p></li></ol><h3 id="what-the-agent-does-not-do" tabindex="-1">What the agent does not do <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-the-agent-does-not-do" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What the agent does not do&quot;">​</a></h3><ul><li>Initializing the repository and connecting a remote are gestures <strong>of the application</strong> (<strong>Settings › Sync</strong>): no tool does them.</li><li>It never forces a push and never commits your changes on its own initiative.</li></ul><h3 id="the-automation-on-every-write" tabindex="-1">The automation on every write <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-automation-on-every-write" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The automation on every write&quot;">​</a></h3><p>When the memory is a git repository and the <strong>Automation</strong> box (&quot;Agents commit and sync automatically&quot;) is ticked, every agent tool that <strong>writes</strong> is wrapped: before writing it checks the remote, after writing it commits what it touched, and only that, then pushes. Your uncommitted changes do not leave with it, except a file you had already modified and that the tool rewrites: that one leaves with the commit, which names it (a <code>Kessho-Embarque</code> line), and the agent must tell you. Read tools do not run git; <code>mem_git_sync</code>, <code>mem_git_commit</code> and <code>mem_git_resolve</code> sit outside this wrapping because they are the gesture themselves.</p><p>The commit&#39;s first line is composed by the <strong>Automation</strong> origin&#39;s template (by default <code>kessho({outil}): {objet} {demande}</code>); the body, which lists the files, and the <code>Kessho-Outil</code>, <code>Kessho-Embarque</code> and <code>Kessho-Repris</code> lines always stay, whatever the template.</p><p>The result of a writing tool then carries a sync report, which the agent reads every time:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th><code>statut</code></th><th>What it means</th><th>What the agent does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>synchronise</code></td><td>Committed and pushed.</td><td>Nothing.</td></tr><tr><td><code>commite_local</code></td><td>Committed here, not pushed (offline, no remote, authentication refused…).</td><td>Tells you in one line, with the reason.</td></tr><tr><td><code>en_attente</code></td><td>Committed, but your uncommitted changes prevent updating from the remote.</td><td>Tells you and <strong>proposes</strong> <code>mem_git_commit</code> once you have reviewed them.</td></tr><tr><td><code>ignore</code></td><td>Git did nothing (automation off, no repository, git not found…).</td><td>Nothing, except relaying git&#39;s installation command.</td></tr><tr><td><code>conflit</code></td><td>A real conflict: the rebase is aborted and <strong>the tool wrote nothing</strong>.</td><td>Stops and goes through <code>mem_git_resolve</code>, with your agreement.</td></tr><tr><td><code>erreur</code></td><td>Repository busy, an operation already in progress, or a recovery that could not be completed.</td><td>Tells you and retries a moment later; it never deletes <code>index.lock</code>.</td></tr></tbody></table><h3 id="the-commit-message-template" tabindex="-1">The commit message template <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-commit-message-template" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The commit message template&quot;">​</a></h3><p>In <a href="/en/guide/sync.html#compose-your-commit-messages">Settings › Sync</a>, each commit origin has a message template. Two concern the agent:</p><ul><li><strong>AI agent</strong>: for <code>mem_git_commit</code>, the default template is <strong>Agent&#39;s message</strong>, <code>{message}</code>. The agent&#39;s message is always kept: it is trimmed, an empty or blank message is refused before any rendering, and the agent&#39;s template can only surround it (it must contain <code>{message}</code>, otherwise it is ignored with a warning). An agent&#39;s subject is never cut.</li><li><strong>Automation</strong>: the template only sets the first line of each writing tool&#39;s commit.</li></ul><p>The message of <code>mem_git_resolve</code> is never affected.</p><p>What the agent can observe: when the chosen template could not be used (missing, refused, without <code>{message}</code>, or an unreadable configuration), Kessho commits with the agent&#39;s message as is, and the <code>mem_git_commit</code> response carries a <code>signalements</code> list that says why. With no fallback, that field is absent: the response keeps its usual shape. For a writing tool, the same warning appears in the sync report. The agent relays these warnings to you; it never changes the templates, which are set in the application. The content variables (<code>{zones}</code>, <code>{projets}</code>, <code>{fichiers}</code>) and <code>{appareil}</code> write into the git history, pushed to your remote repository: no supplied template contains them.</p><p>The agent does not call <code>mem_git_commit</code> on its own: it does so when you ask, or when it judges it useful and with your agreement.</p><h2 id="what-you-see" tabindex="-1">What you see <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-you-see" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What you see&quot;">​</a></h2>',17)),o(h,{name:"synchro",alt:"Memory synchronization in the settings: files changed since the last commit and a commit message being typed."}),e[4]||(e[4]=i('<p>The application shows the same repository, and it is the one that does in one click what <code>mem_git_sync</code> does through the agent: the <strong>Sync the memory</strong> button in the status bar commits, pulls, then pushes. The <strong>GIT</strong> field says where the repository stands (up to date, N to push, N to pull, conflict…), and <strong>Settings › Sync</strong> carries the <strong>Sync</strong>, <strong>Pull</strong> and <strong>Push</strong> actions, the <strong>Commit message</strong>, the remote, the <strong>Automation</strong> box and the <strong>Commit messages</strong> (one template per origin). Everything is described in <a href="/en/guide/sync.html">Sync</a>.</p><p>One difference worth knowing: the button commits <strong>everything</strong> that changed under the message composed by the <strong>One click</strong> origin&#39;s template (by default &quot;vault backup: …&quot;), whereas the agent only commits your changes with your agreement.</p>',2))])}const v=d(l,[["render",g]]);export{b as __pageData,v as default};
